---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import RfDevice from '../../components/RfDevice.astro';
// Generated on every build from the array the profile is COMPILED from, so
// this page and the shipped bundle cannot disagree (roadmap 95.1).
import rfProfile from '../../data/rf-profile.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: RF goods receipt" description="Receiving a delivery on a handheld scanner, one-handed and often gloved, with every expected line accounted for before the truck leaves.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: a warehouse floor worker, one-handed and
    often gloved, holding a scanner instead of a mouse. <strong>What
    "done" looks like</strong>: every expected line accounted for — received
    in full, received partial, or explicitly not received — before the
    receipt posts. A GR (goods receipt) screen driven by a handheld RF
    scanner — the
    scanner types the barcode fast and sends Enter, so the field needs to stay
    focused and clear itself between scans. Composed from <code>data-scan-input</code>
    (new opt-in behavior, no new CSS — a plain <code>.bo-input</code>), the existing
    <code>.bo-quantity</code> at <code>data-density="spacious"</code> for a
    warehouse-floor large target, and the data table for the receiving log. A
    visually-hidden <code>aria-live="polite"</code> region (linked via the field's
    own <code>aria-describedby</code>) announces each successful scan — a
    screen-reader/low-vision RF user gets non-visual confirmation without it
    being read for every sighted user.
    <strong>Not for a desk-based receiving clerk keying a paper packing slip.</strong>
    Without a physical scanner there is no barcode to feed the always-focused field, the
    spacious steppers just waste screen space, and the workflow is really "type these
    numbers in and check them" — that's
    <a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>a detail form</a> at normal density, not this
    pattern.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The screen — try it</h2>
    <p>Shown the way the scanner gets it: an isolated 360×640 document styled
    by ONLY the <code>rf-essentials</code> profile (roadmap 59.2,
    Chrome/WebView&nbsp;108) — not the docs bundle. This is where "separated
    and used only on RF scanner / mobile / tablet" is actually demonstrated:
    the profile is what's separated (a build target), not the markup or the
    components. One screen, not two — this page rendered the same component a
    second time at desktop width until roadmap 135.1, which is a size no
    receiving clerk holds. (131.1 removed that duplicate from the other three
    RF screens and wrongly spared this one, on the belief that its top demo
    showed something different. It did not: both rendered
    <code>&lt;ScanToReceive /&gt;</code>, and this page said so itself.)</p>
    <p><strong>What is in the profile — all {rfProfile.count} of them.</strong>
    Generated from the same array the bundle is compiled from, so this list
    and what ships cannot disagree. Everything else in the framework is
    deliberately absent: a scanner runs a few screens, so the profile carries
    what those screens need and nothing more. A component missing from this
    list is a scope statement, not a gap.</p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      {rfProfile.components.map((c) => <li><a href={base + c.href}><code>{c.name}</code></a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <RfDevice src={base + '/patterns/rf/goods-receipt-rf/'} title="RF scanner, rf-essentials profile at 360×640 — starts on goods receipt, and its links walk the whole track" />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Same scan-clear-refocus behavior, same
    <code>data-density="spacious"</code> steppers — <code>initScanInput()</code>
    and <code>initQuantity()</code> are JS, unaffected by which CSS profile
    loads (the RF floor research found JS already safe back to Chrome 80;
    this profile is the CSS half of the ask). Try it: click into the field
    and type a barcode, then Enter.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Scan feedback — a cue for someone watching the rack</h2>
    <p>Type anything into the screen above and press Enter: the
    <strong>device's</strong> viewport flashes green for ~600ms — the flash is
    <code>position: fixed</code>, so it covers whatever viewport the screen is
    running in, which here is the handheld rather than this page. Scan a code starting with
    <code>REJECT</code> and it flashes red with the reason announced. A
    scanner user's eyes are on the shelf, not the screen — the flash is
    readable in peripheral vision, and it is deliberately <strong>not the
    only channel</strong>: every capture and every rejection also lands in
    the <code>data-scan-status</code> live region this page already
    links. Capture is not validity — <code>data-scan-flash</code> makes
    the behavior paint <em>ok</em> when the terminator arrives, and your
    validation calls <code>flashScanResult('error', reason)</code> when a
    captured code is wrong. Under reduced motion the flash is a static
    wash; under forced colors it becomes a Highlight frame, because a
    repainted background would vanish exactly when contrast matters
    most.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<input class="bo-input bo-input--code" data-scan-input autofocus
    aria-describedby="scan-status" />
<p id="scan-status" data-scan-status aria-live="polite" class="bo-visually-hidden"></p>

<script type="module">
  import { initScanInput, flashScanResult } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
  initScanInput(); // once — clears + refocuses on Enter, fires bo:scan,
                    // announces "Scanned <value>" in data-scan-status if linked;
                    // with data-scan-flash on the input, capture also paints
                    // the ok flash. Your validation:
                    //   flashScanResult('error', 'Not on this PO')
  document.querySelector('[data-scan-input]')
    .addEventListener('bo:scan', (e) => console.log(e.detail.value));
</script>`}</code></pre>
  </section>


  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Scan field</strong> — always focused; a hardware scanner types then presses Enter, which <code>initScanInput()</code> treats as a submit.</li>
      <li><strong>Expected vs received list</strong> <span class="bo-u-text-muted">(the demo above logs scans as a flat list; a real screen pre-seeds one row per expected line — barcode, ordered qty, received-so-far — from the PO, and each scan updates its own row instead of appending a new one)</span> — every line with its ordered quantity and a running received count.</li>
      <li><strong>Quantity control</strong> — spacious density and big steppers: this screen is used one-handed, often gloved.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm bar</strong> <span class="bo-u-text-muted">(not in this demo — the live scan log has no post action)</span> — post the receipt; disabled until at least one line has a quantity.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /receipts/:id/scan</td><td>the matched line fragment with its updated count; 404 → an unknown-barcode alert that does NOT steal focus from the scan field</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /receipts/:id</td><td>the posted receipt; 422 for over-receipt beyond tolerance</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /receipts/:id</td><td>resume a partially entered receipt — warehouse sessions get interrupted</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>No scanner? The camera is the
    fallback, as a recipe.</strong> On a consumer phone, add
    <code>capture="environment"</code> to a
    <a href={base + '/components/file-upload'}>file input</a> for photo
    evidence (damage, labels) — file upload's own camera section spells
    out the two-input shape, because a lone <code>capture</code> input
    takes the gallery away, and for live barcode reads feed the
    <code>BarcodeDetector</code> API's result into the same
    <code>bo:scan</code> flow where supported — the screen's contract does
    not change, only the way the string arrives. Deliberately a recipe:
    camera pipelines are a device capability, not framework CSS.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Waiting for scan</td><td>the field holds focus permanently; every state change must return focus to it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unknown barcode</td><td>an alert plus an audible or visible cue — the user is looking at the goods, not the screen</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Over-receipt</td><td>a warning with the tolerance stated; block only past the policy limit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Partial receipt</td><td>the line shows received/ordered; posting a partial is normal and must not warn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Complete</td><td>a clear terminal state — the next scan starts a NEW receipt rather than silently appending</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Session lost / flaky wifi</td><td>the RECONNECT state: on regaining the server, re-render as the server has it. Session persistence is INFRASTRUCTURE (the reference products sell it as a server), not a screen promise — an unsent scan is retyped, which is honest and cheaper than a silently dropped one that reads as saved</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>the scan field, quantity input, and table render as plain, focusable, typable native controls, but the core flow cannot complete: <code>initScanInput()</code> is what listens for the terminator key, clears/refocuses the field, and dispatches <code>bo:scan</code> — without it, pressing Enter after a scan does nothing, so no line ever reaches the log. The <code>+</code>/&minus; quantity steppers also need <code>initQuantity()</code>, though the number input itself stays usable via keyboard/typing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 3 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/quantity'}>Quantity</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/concepts/density'}>Density (spacious)</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ["/concepts/js-behaviors", "JS behaviors"],
    ["/components/quantity", "Quantity"],
    ["/components/data-table", "Data table"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
